<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eberhard I, Count of Württemberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1265">1265</a>–<a href="/years/1325">1325</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Eberhard the Illustrious</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eberhard I, called the Illustrious, was Count of Württemberg from 1279 to 1325. He fought the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the Swabian imperial cities, and lost most of his lands in 1312 to forces acting for Emperor Henry VII, but recovered them after the emperor's death. He made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> the residence of the counts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eberhard-i-count-of-wurttemberg.jpg" alt="Eberhard I, Count of Württemberg" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christian Friedrich Sattler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eberhard_I_vW_Sattler.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eberhard was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> on 13 March 1265, the son of Count Ulrich I of Württemberg, who died a few weeks before his birth. His mother, Agnes of Silesia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a>, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-ii-the-bald">Bolesław II Rogatka</a>, probably died at his birth; some sources speak of a caesarean section. His half-brother Ulrich II succeeded their father at about eleven years old, generally assumed to be under the guardianship of Count Hartmann I of Grüningen. Ulrich II died in 1279 and Hartmann in 1280, leaving Eberhard to rule the county without restraint from 1280.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ulrich I had enlarged Württemberg with conquests that the anti-king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-raspe">Henry Raspe</a> had legitimised, but after Rudolph I was elected king these lands had to be returned to the Empire. Rudolph set up bailiwicks to govern them, made his brother-in-law Albert II of Hohenberg-Rotenburg vogt of Lower Swabia, and named his young son Rudolph II duke of Swabia, hoping to restore the duchy left leaderless since the execution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conradin">Conradin</a>, the last of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a>, in 1268. Eberhard resisted, and after Rudolph's death in 1291 he won military successes against Albert of Hohenberg.</p><p>Adolph of Nassau did not press into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>. After Adolph's overthrow in 1298 Eberhard backed Albert I, who in return made him vogt of Lower Swabia, a post he used to secure his own claims, until fighting with Albert broke out again in 1305. Eberhard supported the Bohemian nobility against Albert and his successor Henry VII, and Vogt Konrad IV von Weinberg, acting on Henry's orders, made war on him.</p><p>The death of Henry VII on 24 August 1313 and the double election of 1314, which set Louis IV against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-fair">Frederick the Fair</a>, saved Württemberg. Moving between the two kings, Eberhard made good his losses and gained more, and with money from the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> he bought land and towns from impoverished noble families such as the count palatine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Eberhard made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> the capital of Württemberg. He died there on 5 June 1325 and was buried in the city's Stiftskirche. He married three times: a first wife of uncertain identity, possibly of the Werdenberg family, then Margaret, a daughter of Duke Frederick III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, and finally Irmengard, a daughter of Margrave Rudolph I of Baden-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>. His son by Margaret, Ulrich III, succeeded him and ruled until 1344. The clergyman Ulrich von Höfingen is believed to have been his illegitimate son.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eberhard I, Count of Württemberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Eberhard I</b> fought the Swabian imperial cities and Emperor Henry VII, and Stuttgart became the residence in the 14th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-wurttemberg">County of Württemberg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.78,9.18&amp;year=1304" aria-label="Stuttgart on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4762 460 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Eberhard I, Count of Württemberg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5212 730h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5212 730h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.78,9.18&amp;year=1304">What was here in 1304? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
